==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|ang|ih}}

===Etymology===
{{proto|Germanic|ek||ik|I|lang=}}, from Indo-European ''*egom'' ‘I’, from a presumed form ''*eg''.  Germanic cognates include Old Frisian ''[[ik]]'', Old Saxon ''[[ik]]'' (Dutch ''[[ik]]''), Old High German ''[[ih]]'' (German ''[[ich]]''), Old Norse ''[[ek]]'' (Swedish ''[[jag]]''), Gothic '''[[𐌹𐌺]]'''.  The Indo-European root, in various forms, is also the source of Sanskrit [[अहम्]], Latin ''[[ego]]'' (Spanish ''[[yo]]'' etc.), Ancient Greek {{term|lang=grc|ἐγώ}}, Lithuanian ''[[aš]]'', Avestan ''azəm'', Old Church Slavonic ''az’'' (Russian ''[[я]]''), Latvian ''[[es]]'', Old Armenian {{term|ես||sc=Armn|tr=es|lang=xcl}}.  For declined derivations, see under ''[[me#Old English|mē]]'', ''[[we#Old English|wē]]'' etc.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/iʧ/|lang=ang}}

===Pronoun===
'''iċ'''

# [[I]], used by the speaker referring to himself or herself as the subject, or in agreement with that subject

